The Tyrants fall into three categories, and the pattern is now clear. The First Tyrant, the Black Fire, was a Wengari of the Shadow Paws. He was a good king for 150 years, and then the madness took him. His parasitic Aura flame consumed his victims. He died when his body could no longer contain the energy. The Third Tyrant, Uthgar Lightbringer, was a Bright Paw who trained under the Deep and made a pact with the Tree.

He vanished into the Chamber of Reflection. He did not go mad. He did not burn. He simply disappeared. The Fifth Tyrant, Velarius Vane, was a Humman who discovered the March's poisons could be used as medicine. Something changed in him around his thirtieth year. He created the Plague.

The First and the Fifth both fell to madness. They are also the two Tyrants most directly connected to the Rune-Belt. The First Tyrant's capital was in the desert, but his madness—the parasitic fire that consumed everything—is the signature of something that twists natural power into annihilation. Velarius went into the Flickermarch and came back changed. Something in the Rune-Belt wounded him, or woke him, or claimed him.

The Deep and the Tree both despise the entity that dwells there. They have despised it since before the Wengari walked the desert. They despise it for reasons neither of them has fully explained to Ajani. The Deep hates the entity because it scarred his people twice now. The Threx remember the Black Fire. The Threx remember the Plague. Both Tyrants who fell to madness were touched by the thing in the Rune-Belt, and both times, the Threx suffered.

The Tree hates the entity for reasons still unknown. That is the gap you have left, and it is a deliberate gap. The Tree's ancient memory holds something it has not shared. The Third Tyrant, Uthgar, is the hinge. He trained under the Deep in the marshes. He made a pact with the Tree in the desert. He built a road that was meant to connect them, but he vanished before it could be completed. He did not go mad.

He was not consumed. He simply disappeared into the Chamber of Reflection, and no one has ever found him. The entity in the Rune-Belt tried to claim him and failed. That is why the Deep and the Tree both chose to work with him. That is why they are willing to work with Ajani now. They see in him another chance to bridge the gap Uthgar left behind, and they see in him a potential weapon against the entity that took two Tyrants already.

Your Fourth Tyrant, Xal'thyra, was a victim of her own power. She could resonate with other beings, hear their inner songs. The cacophony drove her mad. That is not the entity's doing; it is a tragic consequence of her own gift.

Your Second Tyrant, Crr'zzak, was not mad at all. He was a Chi'Thak who did exactly what he intended to do. He organized the swarm, fed his colony, passed on his genetic legacy. He is a cultural hero to his people. No super-organism touched him. No entity influenced him. He was simply a Convergence-born fulfilling his purpose.

The Dragari have been walking toward the capital for months. They are the oldest race. They carry the oldest memories. They know things that even the Deep and the Tree have forgotten. They have been observing Ajani, watching him, waiting to see what kind of White Dawn he will become. They will arrive with a warning about the entity in the Rune-Belt and perhaps a fragment of the Lightbringer's lost history.

The seeds are planted. The Dragari messenger, the lore about the entity, the Deep's hatred, the Tree's silence—all of it is ready to converge.

The Bright Paws moved to the desert later, after the Third Tyrant made the pact with the Kyre Tree. The capital of Styxian was built around the pact, around the Tree's water, around the hanging gardens. The Wengari are not originally a desert people. They are a people of the belt who adapted to the desert through the pact. The Shadow Paws, who stayed closest to their original territory, are the ones who carry the deepest memory of what was lost.

They are the ones who know what the entity in the Rune-Belt is capable of. They are the ones who gave Ajani the obsidian dagger forged in the First Tyrant's fall. That is why the First Tyrant fell. He was a Shadow Paw, ruling from the Rune-Belt, and the entity reached him. It twisted his power into the parasitic fire that consumed him and half his people. The Black Fire was not merely a Tyrant's madness.

It was the entity's first attempt to claim a White Dawn. It succeeded.

The Deep's hatred now makes more sense. The Threx of the Flickermarch were scarred by the Black Fire. The entity used a Wengari to burn the Deep's children. That is why the Deep despises the entity. The Fifth Tyrant, Velarius, was the second attempt. He was a Humman, not a Wengari, but he was Convergence-born. The entity reached him, twisted his healing into the Plague, and used him to scar the world a second time.

Two Tyrants fell to madness. Both were touched by the entity in the Rune-Belt. The Tree's hatred of the entity is still unexplained, but the Deep's hatred now has a clear source. The Threx were burned twice. The First Tyrant's fire and the Fifth Tyrant's plague both left scars on the mycelial network. The Deep remembers both. The Dragari have been observing Ajani for months, watching to see whether this White Dawn will also be claimed by the entity, and they are about to arrive with their warning.

