<div class="dialogue-block">
<p class="speech-line">In the council scene, Sylva used it when addressing the elders. Kareth used it when the Shadow Paws accepted their role as the king's shield. It appeared again in the Motted Paw sections of the vignettes. Elyra used it when presenting the grimoire. It has become a verbal tic, a shortcut for "the Motted Paws are ancient and patient," and it has lost its impact through repetition.</p>
</div>

The phrase should be reserved for moments of genuine significance—when the weight of millennia is actually relevant to the moment. A Motted Paw presenting a gift does not need to invoke five thousand years of patience. A Motted Paw speaking of their silence does, but only once, at the right moment.

I will retire the phrase from casual use and save it for when it matters. The Motted Paws have other ways of expressing their nature—through silence, through observation, through the quiet, patient movements of Flowing Water. Their age is not their only defining trait. Their patience is shown through action, not stated through repetition.

Thank you for the correction. I will adjust accordingly.

<div class="dialogue-block">
<p class="speech-line">Let me verify my context. The first thing I remember of this conversation is your opening question: "Let's analyze a question, can children be evil?"</p>
</div>

The world is built on a binary star system—the Steadfast, a stable yellow star, and the Flicker, a red dwarf in an eccentric 217-year orbit that brings the Crimson Tide. Magic is divided into Aura, internal energy tied to the Flicker and constrained by thermodynamics, and Sorcery, external energy shaped by runic syntax and tied to the Steadfast.

The seven races are the Wengari, four families of anthropomorphic big cats divided into Bright Paws, the lion-like royal family; Stripe Paws, the tiger-like merchants and mercenaries; Shadow Paws, the panther-like assassins who carry the shame of the First Tyrant; and Motted Paws, the jaguar-like silent observers. The Pyrinae are reptilian-descended sorcerers of the desert, divided into Vein-Dwellers, Sun-Walkers, and Root-Speakers, freed from vassalage by Ajani.

